WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:02.910
In this section of the course,

2
00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.929
we're gonna go over Nanite and Virtual Shadow Maps.

3
00:00:05.929 --> 00:00:09.119
So let's go ahead and get started.

4
00:00:09.119 --> 00:00:12.779
So many people will ask, what is Nanite exactly?

5
00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:17.260
Well, it's Unreal 5's virtualized geometry system.

6
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.839
So it's stored as a hierarchical system

7
00:00:19.839 --> 00:00:22.480
of smaller parts using triangles.

8
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.039
It adds geometric complexity with less issues.

9
00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:27.699
So you can think of it as it's drawing

10
00:00:27.699 --> 00:00:30.239
the geometry complexity as needed

11
00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:32.359
and putting the rest in a catch.

12
00:00:32.359 --> 00:00:35.619
Nanite allows for higher visual fidelity

13
00:00:35.619 --> 00:00:37.359
and works well with Lumen.

14
00:00:37.359 --> 00:00:39.439
So it's ideal to pair those up.

15
00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:42.240
Outside of Lumen, the generating of shadow maps,

16
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.960
such as baking, will take too long on a complicated mesh.

17
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.100
So you have to be careful with it.

18
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.039
Doesn't mean you can't use Nanite

19
00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:51.520
within the old school baking method.

20
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.460
It's just not ideal, especially when you can get

21
00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:56.399
the performance you get out of Lumen

22
00:00:56.399 --> 00:00:59.020
and you can update your lighting on the fly.

23
00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:02.340
Virtual shadow maps are used for more realistic results.

24
00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.579
So virtual shadow maps pairs with Nanite

25
00:01:04.579 --> 00:01:05.920
and pairs with Lumen.

26
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.579
Again, you can think of it as the three musketeers.

27
00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:11.540
They're the best approaches for using the,

28
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.780
they're getting the best visual fidelity you can

29
00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:18.650
and getting it updated quickly and efficiently.

30
00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:21.269
Instance, object, count and draw calls

31
00:01:21.269 --> 00:01:24.370
are less of a limitation when you're using this system.

32
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:27.129
Now instances are in the millions.

33
00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:31.290
Auto LODs and streaming uses clusters of data.

34
00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.250
So it's kind of like virtual texturing.

35
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:36.109
Virtual texturing calls upon the textures,

36
00:01:36.109 --> 00:01:37.930
the higher resolution when needed

37
00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:40.530
based on the position of yourself in the world.

38
00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.489
The same thing with Nanite.

39
00:01:42.489 --> 00:01:46.569
It calls upon the higher visual fidelity of that object

40
00:01:46.569 --> 00:01:50.250
when you are positioned in the right spot.

41
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.629
So it's not gonna be drawing it all the time.

42
00:01:52.629 --> 00:01:55.269
So visually and efficiently working with Lumen

43
00:01:55.269 --> 00:01:56.810
and virtual shadow maps,

44
00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:58.209
it works great.

45
00:01:58.209 --> 00:02:00.290
So you'll see down below the last note here

46
00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.549
is more visually accurate than displacement

47
00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:04.569
with less shadow artifacts.

48
00:02:04.569 --> 00:02:06.709
So let's talk about that for a second.

49
00:02:06.709 --> 00:02:08.889
So displacement is inaccurate

50
00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:11.110
with extreme shadowing changes

51
00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.569
and light changes with higher displacement values

52
00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:17.270
due to going beyond the object's original bounds.

53
00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:20.389
Now you'll notice every object has a bounds limit.

54
00:02:20.389 --> 00:02:21.710
If you simply type that in,

55
00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:23.349
in the search and the details,

56
00:02:23.349 --> 00:02:24.689
you will find the bounds.

57
00:02:24.689 --> 00:02:26.530
Let me show you just for a second.

58
00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.250
So you see in Unreal, I've selected this object.

59
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.340
If I simply type in bounds,

60
00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.719
you'll see that there's a bound scale.

61
00:02:34.719 --> 00:02:37.379
So if I had more surface detail on here

62
00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:38.939
and a displacement active,

63
00:02:38.939 --> 00:02:42.099
I would have to increase this if I'm noticing

64
00:02:42.099 --> 00:02:45.580
that there's maybe some graphical weird issues going on,

65
00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.060
or I just want that displacement

66
00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.379
to be a little bit more accurate.

67
00:02:50.379 --> 00:02:53.419
And what I mean by graphical distortion

68
00:02:53.419 --> 00:02:55.699
is sometimes when you put a displacement

69
00:02:55.699 --> 00:02:58.419
and the bounds isn't high enough,

70
00:02:58.419 --> 00:03:01.979
it won't know how to draw the surface new shape

71
00:03:01.979 --> 00:03:04.099
that you've put in here in the material

72
00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:05.659
versus the original shape.

73
00:03:05.659 --> 00:03:08.219
That's what the bounds scale comes in for.

74
00:03:08.219 --> 00:03:10.539
So the shadows can become inaccurate

75
00:03:10.539 --> 00:03:11.740
with that surface detail.

76
00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:13.699
I don't have a high surface detail

77
00:03:13.699 --> 00:03:15.439
with a displacement active,

78
00:03:15.439 --> 00:03:17.419
but you kind of get the point.

79
00:03:17.419 --> 00:03:21.139
So you want to avoid those weird artifacts

80
00:03:21.139 --> 00:03:23.419
and the shadows that can happen when using displacement.

81
00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:26.069
So Nanite's there to kind of solve that.

82
00:03:26.069 --> 00:03:28.669
Nanite is great for high resolution model shadowing

83
00:03:29.629 --> 00:03:33.069
and it keeps accuracy working with the virtual shadow maps.

84
00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:34.689
You're going to see off to the right here,

85
00:03:34.689 --> 00:03:39.689
two demonstrations, two images that show that in action.

86
00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.550
Here's kind of like a default,

87
00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.909
trying to use a displacement.

88
00:03:43.909 --> 00:03:47.590
And you know, maybe not as much detail on this model.

89
00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:49.110
Here we have a Nanite object

90
00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.349
using virtual shadow maps on the bottom

91
00:03:51.349 --> 00:03:54.110
and you'll see it looks much, much better.

92
00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:56.500
It looks really, really great.

93
00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:01.039
You can also use Nanite for ICV effects if you need to.

94
00:04:01.039 --> 00:04:03.800
So Nanite now supports explicit tangents

95
00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:05.680
in the data format and runtime.

96
00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.759
So you're going to get more accuracy.

97
00:04:07.759 --> 00:04:10.960
So before Nanite used to rely on a tangent space

98
00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:13.639
that is implicitly derived in the material.

99
00:04:13.639 --> 00:04:16.120
So it would be strictly in the material front

100
00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:19.670
based on the screen space position and UV gradients.

101
00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:22.610
But now things are more explicit with the tangents

102
00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:24.189
and the data format and runtime.

103
00:04:24.189 --> 00:04:28.829
So it looks a lot better, supports those tangents.

104
00:04:28.829 --> 00:04:30.790
Now let's take a look at using Nanite

105
00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:31.949
and how to set it up.

106
00:04:31.949 --> 00:04:33.870
It is really super easy.

107
00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.949
And I'm going to show you some search options

108
00:04:36.949 --> 00:04:38.509
that you can do to test

109
00:04:38.509 --> 00:04:41.569
and see what things are not Nanite active,

110
00:04:41.569 --> 00:04:45.790
quick and easy in the content browser.

111
00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:47.569
Speaking of in the content browser,

112
00:04:47.569 --> 00:04:49.949
at any time you can select any piece of geometry,

113
00:04:49.949 --> 00:04:52.790
right click Nanite and activate it.

114
00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:54.470
Let me show you real quick how to do it.

115
00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:56.899
It's pretty simple.

116
00:04:56.899 --> 00:04:59.620
So in Unreal, you'll see I have a piece of geometry

117
00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:00.579
selected.

118
00:05:00.579 --> 00:05:02.660
Let's go ahead and go to that folder

119
00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:04.319
and find out where that's at.

