00:05:04.319 --> 00:05:06.379
Now, to be able to check on this piece of geometry,

121
00:05:06.379 --> 00:05:10.500
I could right click and go to Nanite and activate it.

122
00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.180
Let's open up a Nanite scene

123
00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.959
a little bit more detail.

124
00:05:13.959 --> 00:05:15.160
So you can kind of see,

125
00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.439
you can kind of see that workflow.

126
00:05:19.439 --> 00:05:20.959
In this scene, we have a few rocks

127
00:05:20.959 --> 00:05:22.939
that are a bit more detail in here,

128
00:05:22.939 --> 00:05:26.819
but maybe I've imported them from say, Quixel Bridge,

129
00:05:26.819 --> 00:05:28.699
and I've chosen Nanite as an option.

130
00:05:28.699 --> 00:05:29.879
Yes, in Quixel Bridge,

131
00:05:29.879 --> 00:05:32.139
you can choose Nanite ahead of time.

132
00:05:32.139 --> 00:05:33.420
When you go to import,

133
00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.879
you can import anything at any time.

134
00:05:35.879 --> 00:05:37.300
You can also turn on Nanite

135
00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:40.120
when the import window shows up.

136
00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:42.360
So with this object selected,

137
00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:45.120
again, I can find it in my content browser.

138
00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:49.139
I can right click and go to Nanite and activate Nanite.

139
00:05:49.139 --> 00:05:52.180
You can also double click on the object itself.

140
00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:57.009
And in here, just type in Nanite,

141
00:05:57.009 --> 00:05:59.470
and we can make sure that we enable Nanite support.

142
00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:02.790
This is gonna make sure that this object is correctly

143
00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:06.800
being rendered using that Nanite data.

144
00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:10.240
Now, in Unreal,

145
00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:12.680
normally in the old school way of doing things,

146
00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:16.199
we would have a method called LODs or level of detail

147
00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:18.000
that's connected to geometry.

148
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.319
That occurs when Nanite isn't active.

149
00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:22.160
If I was to turn Nanite off,

150
00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.600
it would make it so that now we have a level of detail

151
00:06:24.639 --> 00:06:26.839
based on camera distance.

152
00:06:26.839 --> 00:06:30.139
But now with Nanite, our geometry is put into a catch

153
00:06:30.139 --> 00:06:32.639
so that it's only drawing the high fidelity

154
00:06:32.639 --> 00:06:36.250
when needed based on that distance.

155
00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:39.589
Note in the number three here of my points,

156
00:06:39.589 --> 00:06:44.569
if using chaos, active also in geo,

